{
  "dom_id": "step-q345095",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=-2{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2t)+\\frac{12}{13}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t).\n$$\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is\n{{field-1}}\namperes.",
  "worked_solution": "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(-2{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2t)+\\frac{12}{13}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t)) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{10}{13}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t)+\\frac{24}{13}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=2{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-3t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{13}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t)+\\frac{24}{13}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)\u2248{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{13}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t)+\\frac{24}{13}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{10}{13})}^{2}+{(\\frac{24}{13})}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{676}{169}}=2.\n$$",
